  "result": "Done. All four tests in `tests/test_t1.py` pass with `python -m pytest -q`.\n\n**What I built** in `catalog/service.py`:\n- A `NotFound` exception and a `Catalog` class with create, get, update, delete, and list.\n- Records live in a plain dict, which preserves insertion order, so listing returns creation order for free.\n- Get and list return copies, so callers cannot mutate internal state by accident.\n- Update rejects any field other than title or author with a ValueError before touching the record.\n\n**Approach chosen:** I picked Approach A, sequential integers from a per-catalog counter. The spec describes a single-process in-memory store where ids are only ever minted by the catalog itself, so UUIDs would add no coordination benefit while giving up short, sortable, human-readable ids. Ids are never reused after deletion, so they stay unique and stable for a record's lifetime.",
